<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malik-i-Maidan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1549</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malik-i-Maidan, 'monarch of the plains', is a huge bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> on a bastion of the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>. It was cast in 1549 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> by Muhammad bin Hasan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> and later brought to Bijapur, where it became the most famous gun of the Adil Shahi fortifications. Weighing about 55 tonnes, it is among the largest cast bronze guns of the early modern period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malik-i-maidan.jpg" alt="Malik-i-Maidan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hinton, Henry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Part_of_Fort,_Beejapore..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
